title: Using simplified queries
tags: [[Simplified query language]]
kind: guide
summary: Typing simplified queries in the GUI's query box and with -s on the command line, and seeing the DSL they become.

!! In the GUI

The query box of the `metarecord-list` panel takes simplified text; focus it with
<<key "metarecord-list:focus simplified" Ctrl+/>> (or <<key "metarecord-list:focus simplified" "e s">>).
''Show normal DSL'' reveals what it expands to, updated as you type. To edit the DSL
by hand, ''freeze'' it: the DSL box then drives the query and the simplified text no
longer does, until you focus the simplified box again, which unfreezes it. An
expansion error shows under the simplified box; a DSL error under the DSL box.

!! On the command line

`-s` reads the `-q` selector as simplified text, expanding it locally before the query
is sent:

```
mf -n music metarecord -q 'genre=jazz rating>=4' -s get
mf -n music metarecord -q '#jazz modified younger 3d' -s get --count
```

!! With the default grammar

|!You type |!It becomes |
|`genre=jazz rating>3` |`genre = "jazz" AND rating > 3` |
|`genre=jazz OR genre=blues`, `genre=jazz + genre=blues` |`… OR …` |
|`!fav` |`NOT rating >= 4` |
|`rating=4`, `seen=true` |`rating = 4`, `seen = true` — a number or a boolean stays one |
|`title~live` |`title MATCHES "live"` |
|`p osm "scien fic"`, `title osmd blue` |`osm(mfr_path, "scien fic")`, `osmd(title, "blue")` |
|`rating?`, `rating!?`, `rating??` |`IS PRESENT`, `IS ABSENT`, `IS UNKNOWN` |
|`size>=100MB`, `length<5mins` |`mfr_size >= 104857600`, `length < 300` |
|`under "/music"` (quoted: a `/` is not part of a word) |`mfr_path ->* "/music"` |
|`#jazz`, `#=music/jazz`, `##=music/jazz` |the tag macros ([[Tag syntax in simplified queries]]) |
|`modified younger 3d` |a date macro ([[Dates in simplified queries]]) |
|`3f2a…9b`, `tag->3f2a…9b` |that metarecord; the records pointing at it |

Words separated by spaces mean `AND`; `OR` or `+` separate alternatives; `!` negates;
parentheses group; an empty query matches everything. `p` is the file path
(`mfr_path`), `size` is `mfr_size`, any other name passes through — so `path` is the
tag hierarchy's field. The whole list: [[The default grammar]].
